\title{Tame almost complex structures adapted to symplectic\\
simple normal crossings divisors}
\author{}
\date{}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent Let $(M,\omega)$ be a closed symplectic manifold and $D\subset M$
a symplectic simple normal crossings divisor. We prove that there exists
an $\omega$-tame almost complex structure $J$ such that every point of $M$
has a neighbourhood with smooth complex coordinates $(z_1,\dots,z_n)$ in
which, for some $0\le r\le n$, the functions $z_1,\dots,z_r$ are
$J$-holomorphic and $D$ is given by $z_1\cdots z_r=0$. No deformation of
$\omega$ is needed: the constant path $\omega_t=\omega$ works.
\end{abstract}

\section{Statement and overview}\label{sec:statement}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $(M,\omega)$ be a closed symplectic manifold of dimension $2n$ and let
$D\subset M$ be a symplectic simple normal crossings divisor. Then there
exists a smooth $\omega$-tame almost complex structure $J$ on $M$ such
that every point of $M$ has a neighbourhood $U$ with smooth complex
coordinates $(z_1,\dots,z_n)\colon U\to U'\subset\C^n$ satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\normalfont(i)] $D\cap U=\{z_1\cdots z_r=0\}$ for some $0\le r\le n$;
\item[\normalfont(ii)] $z_1,\dots,z_r$ are $J|_U$-holomorphic, i.e.\
$\dd z_i\circ J=i\,\dd z_i$ on $U$ for all $i\le r$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular the answer to the question is \emph{yes}, and the optional
deformation is unnecessary: one may take the constant path
$\omega_t\equiv\omega$ (a path of cohomologous symplectic forms along
which $D$ remains a symplectic simple normal crossings divisor).
\end{theorem}

We recall the definition used in the question: $D\subset M$ is a
\emph{symplectic simple normal crossings divisor} if $D$ is a finite union
$\bigcup_{\alpha\in I}D_\alpha$ and every point $p$ has a Darboux chart
$(U,\varphi)$, $\varphi\colon U\to\C^n$ with $\varphi^*\omega_0=\omega$,
whose complex coordinates $(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ satisfy
$D\cap U=\{x_1\cdots x_r=0\}$ for some $0\le r\le n$, the $D_\alpha$
being the coordinate hyperplanes there. The \emph{type} of $p\in D$ is
$r(p)=\#\{\alpha:p\in D_\alpha\}$; the type-$r$ stratum $D^{\{r\}}$ is a
smooth symplectic submanifold of codimension $2r$; in particular $r(p)\le
n$ everywhere.

\textbf{Notation and standard facts.} For a symplectic vector space
$(V,\omega)$, $\mathcal J_\tau(V,\omega)$ is the space of $\omega$-tame
complex structures on $V$; it is an open subset of $\mathrm{End}(V)$ and is
diffeomorphic to a Siegel upper half-space, hence contractible. A smooth
$\C$-valued function $f$ on an open set $W$ is \emph{$J$-holomorphic} if
$\dd f\circ J=i\,\dd f$ pointwise on $W$. We use: Darboux's theorem;
partitions of unity; and the following standard \emph{extension and
smoothing principle}: for a smooth fibre bundle $E\to B$ over a manifold,
with fibres nonempty and contractible, any continuous section defined on a
closed subset $A\subset B$ extends to a continuous section over $B$; if the
section is already smooth on a neighbourhood of $A$, the extension can be
chosen smooth, smooth on $A$-neighbourhood, coinciding there with the
given section. (The contractible-fibre obstruction groups
$H^{i+1}(B,A;\pi_i(F))$ vanish; smoothing is done by embedding the fibre
in a Euclidean space, mollifying, and retracting.)

\textbf{Overview of the proof.} The question asks for \emph{local}
coordinates, so no global integrability is required; the point is to
organise the pointwise conditions so that they never become
overdetermined. Section~\ref{sec:linear} proves the pointwise fiber
lemma: making a family of independent $1$-jets $J$-holomorphic, under a
symplecticity hypothesis on their kernels, describes a nonempty
contractible subset of $\mathcal J_\tau$. Section~\ref{sec:atlas} builds a
good atlas on a neighbourhood of $D$, defines condition loci
$\Omega_{\nu,k}$ (small tubes around the branches, one per chart), and
reduces Theorem~\ref{thm:main} to constructing a tame $J$ making the
relevant coordinate function holomorphic on each locus
(Proposition~\ref{prop:reduce}). The loci are then shrunk once so that
their closures become pairwise disjoint; finally each condition is
installed inside its own Darboux chart by interpolating to the standard
complex structure through tame structures
(Proposition~\ref{prop:global}), using disjointness of the loci to
preserve previously installed conditions.

