\section{Statement and overview}\label{sec:statement}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $(M,\omega)$ be a closed symplectic manifold of dimension $2n$ and
let $D\subset M$ be a symplectic simple normal crossings divisor. Then
there exists a smooth $\omega$-tame almost complex structure $J$ on $M$
such that every point of $M$ has a neighbourhood $U$ with smooth
complex coordinates $(z_1,\dots,z_n)\colon U\to U'\subset\C^n$
satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[\normalfont(i)] $D\cap U=\{z_1\cdots z_r=0\}$ for some
$0\le r\le n$;
\item[\normalfont(ii)] $z_1,\dots,z_r$ are $J|_U$-holomorphic, i.e.\
$\dd z_i\circ J=i\,\dd z_i$ on $U$ for all $i\le r$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular the answer to the question is \emph{yes}, and the
optional deformation is unnecessary: one may take the constant path
$\omega_t\equiv\omega$ (a path of cohomologous symplectic forms along
which $D$ remains a symplectic simple normal crossings divisor).
\end{theorem}

We recall the definition used: $D\subset M$ is a \emph{symplectic
simple normal crossings divisor} (SNC divisor) if
$D=\bigcup_{\alpha\in I}D_\alpha$ (finite union) and every point $p$
has a Darboux chart $(U,\varphi)$, $\varphi\colon U\to\C^n$,
$\varphi^*\omega_0=\omega$, whose complex coordinates
$(x_1,\dots,x_n)$ satisfy $D\cap U=\{x_1\cdots x_r=0\}$ for some
$0\le r\le n$, the $D_\alpha$ being the coordinate hyperplanes there.
The \emph{type} of $p\in D$ is $r(p):=\#\{\alpha:p\in D_\alpha\}$;
the type-$r$ stratum $D^{\{r\}}$ is a smooth symplectic submanifold of
codimension $2r$; in particular $r(p)\le n$ everywhere.

\textbf{Notation and standard facts.} For a symplectic vector space
$(V,\omega)$, $\mathcal J_\tau(V,\omega)$ denotes the space of
$\omega$-tame complex structures; it is open in $\mathrm{End}(V)$ and
diffeomorphic to a Siegel upper half-space, hence contractible. A
smooth $\C$-valued function $f$ on an open set $W$ is
\emph{$J$-holomorphic} if $\dd f\circ J=i\,\dd f$ pointwise. We use
Darboux's theorem; partitions of unity; and the \emph{section/homotopy
principle}: for a smooth fibre bundle over a manifold with nonempty
contractible fibres, sections are nonempty and any two sections over a
contractible base are homotopic through sections (all obstruction
groups vanish).

\textbf{Overview of the proof.} The question is local, so no global
integrability is needed; the content is to organise the pointwise
conditions so that they are never overdetermined.
Section~\ref{sec:linear} proves the pointwise fiber lemma: a family of
independent $1$-jet holomorphicity conditions with symplectic kernels
cuts out a nonempty contractible subset of $\mathcal J_\tau$.
Section~\ref{sec:atlas} constructs a good atlas near $D$ and
introduces pin conditions (``a coordinate function is $J$-holomorphic
on an open locus''). Section~\ref{sec:jets} proves a jet
transversality lemma allowing one to perturb a defining function so
its differential becomes independent of a fixed finite collection of
forms, while keeping its zero set. Section~\ref{sec:loci} carries out
the disjointification: it produces finitely many install patches with
pairwise disjoint closures, each contained in a good chart, each
carrying pin conditions whose total kernel has codimension $\le n$
(so the fiber lemma applies), and which jointly cover the divisor in
the required sense. Section~\ref{sec:install} installs the conditions
patch by patch by interpolating to the chart's standard complex
structure; the kernel bound is what makes the local install possible,
and disjointness of the patches is what lets the installations not
interfere. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

